First, let me time the file quickly to understand local runtime. Actually, let me first write the RED test (the deterministic sustained-contention test), run it against current code to see it fail, then fix.

Design of the RED test — reusing `ContendedClock`:

Call layout (unchanged): per attempt, 5 timed runs small then 5 timed runs doubled; 3 attempts → 30 calls.
- Attempt 1: calls 1-5 small, 6-10 doubled.
- Attempt 2: calls 11-15 small, 16-20 doubled.
- Attempt 3: calls 21-25 small, 26-30 doubled.

Sustained contention shape observed in run 38830911279: every attempt's doubled MEDIAN inflated (best attempt ratio 3.31) → ≥3 of 5 doubled runs inflated per attempt. Model: spike 4 of 5 doubled runs in every attempt:
- Attempt 1 doubled: {6,7,8,9} (10 clean)
- Attempt 2 doubled: {16,17,18,19} (20 clean)
- Attempt 3 doubled: {26,27,28,29} (30 clean)

With the CURRENT median-of-5 statistic: each attempt's doubled median = sorted[2] of [clean, s, s, s, s] = a spiked value → contaminated. 100k cells: real doubled time ~3-4ms + 30ms spike ≈ 33-34ms; small ~1.6-2ms → attempt ratio ~17x → minRatio ≈ 17 → expectLinearGrowth throws → RED.

With the fixed min-of-5 statistic: each attempt's doubled min = clean run → ratio ~2 → GREEN.

The test name and comment should reference the actual CI evidence (runs 38824807299 + 38830911279, PR #1635 validation).

