First, let me time the file quickly to understand local runtime. Actually, let me first write the RED test (the deterministic sustained-contention test), run it against current code to see it fail, then fix.

Design of the RED test — reusing `ContendedClock`:

Call layout (unchanged): per attempt, 5 timed runs small then 5 timed runs doubled; 3 attempts → 30 calls.
- Attempt 1: calls 1-5 small, 6-10 doubled.
- Attempt 2: calls 11-15 small, 16-20 doubled.
- Attempt 3: calls 21-25 small, 26-30 doubled.

Sustained contention shape observed in run 38830911279: every attempt's doubled MEDIAN inflated (best attempt ratio 3.31) → ≥3 of 5 doubled runs inflated per attempt. Model: spike 4 of 5 doubled runs in every attempt:
- Attempt 1 doubled: {6,7,8,9} (10 clean)
- Attempt 2 doubled: {16,17,18,19} (20 clean)
- Attempt 3 doubled: {26,27,28,29} (30 clean)

With the CURRENT median-of-5 statistic: each attempt's doubled median = sorted[2] of [clean, s, s, s, s] = a spiked value → contaminated. 100k cells: real doubled time ~3-4ms + 30ms spike ≈ 33-34ms; small ~1.6-2ms → attempt ratio ~17x → minRatio ≈ 17 → expectLinearGrowth throws → RED.

With the fixed min-of-5 statistic: each attempt's doubled min = clean run → ratio ~2 → GREEN.

The test name and comment should reference the actual CI evidence (runs 38824807299 + 38830911279, PR #1635 validation).

Now the fix — in expectLinearGrowth:
```dart
final small = medianWrapMs(build(baseCells), 80, elapsedUs: elapsedUs);
final doubled = medianWrapMs(build(baseCells * 2), 80, elapsedUs: elapsedUs);
```
→ switch to a min statistic. Add helper:

```dart
/// Fastest wrap time over [runs] executions (one untimed warm-up first).
///
/// The growth assertions sample with the MIN, not the median: scheduler
/// steals can only ADD wall time, so the fastest observed run is the
/// least-contended estimate of the true cost at each size — the same
/// principle that gates across attempts, applied within them (gh-1532
/// follow-up: on a heavily loaded arm64 shard a steal window can span
/// ≥3 of 5 runs in EVERY attempt, inflating every attempt median; the
/// per-size min still finds the clean run).
double minWrapMs(String line, int width, {int runs = 5, ElapsedUs? elapsedUs}) {
  final measure = elapsedUs ?? _stopwatchUs;
  wrapAnsiLine(line, width); // JIT warm-up, untimed
  var min = double.infinity;
  for (var i = 0; i < runs; i++) {
    final t = measure(() => wrapAnsiLine(line, width)) / 1000.0;
    if (t < min) min = t;
  }
  return min;
}
```

And in expectLinearGrowth use minWrapMs. Update dartdocs of expectLinearGrowth + the _growthAttempts doc.

Wait — one subtlety with the maxRatio (flat) guard under the min statistic: with real contention, min-based attempt ratios are LOWER variance than median-based → maxRatio across attempts will be ~2.0-2.2 in practice, comfortably > 1.5. With the constant fake clock: 1.0 → throws TestFailure (degenerate test passes). With ContendedClock preemption test {6,7,8}: attempt 1 min = clean (runs 9,10) → ~2x; attempts 2,3 clean ~2x → min ~2x <3.2 ✓ max ~2x >1.5 ✓. Good.

Also the flat-measurement guard's PURPOSE: reject a measurement where doubling doesn't show. min-based ratios still show real growth when the clock is honest. ✓.

Edge — could min-of-5 make ratio too LOW (false pass of a real regression)? A real quadratic: every doubled run takes ~4x regardless → min ~4x → trips. ✓.

Now — naming: the file header comment says "The growth asserts take several independent attempts and gate on the min ratio (gh-1532)". I'll update: "...gate on the min ratio across attempts AND the per-size min within an attempt (gh-1532 and the PR #1635 validation follow-up)".

Also update the comment in the preemption test? It says "(median-of-5 absorbs single-run spikes, so a sustained steal is the realistic shape)". After the change, the statistic is min-of-5; the {6,7,8} scenario is absorbed by the per-size min directly. The comment must stay truthful: I'll adjust the wording to describe that the per-size min rides out the spike window, and the cross-attempt min covers anything longer. Keep the spiked indices as-is (still valid input; still passes; layout guard intact).

Let me now also double check `medianWrapMs` stays for the absolute tests: yes — '1M visible ASCII cells wrap in < 100ms (median, warm VM)' and the hard-cut one; those stay median (generous absolute headroom per the file header). Only expectLinearGrowth switches.

